---
// The week 1 sheet: twenty-four hours drawn as a vertical timeline, and what
// you mark as something you might change. It keeps nothing.
//
// Why a tool at all on a week that says paper is fine. The lab asks for a
// continuous twenty-four hours written during the day, and the way that goes
// wrong is structural rather than motivational: reconstructed at night, a day
// arrives with holes in it and with the long stretches rounded off. A drawing
// that closes cannot have holes, and one that is filled as the day goes is the
// thing the lab is actually asking for.
//
// What it deliberately does not carry is the third spec line, what you decided
// not to write down and why. That was a section here for two versions, two
// textareas under a heading, and on the page it was the heaviest thing in the
// component: two large empty boxes under a drawing. The line asks what a
// student can say, not what a sheet records, and the lab already does it in
// the room, where the days are read back to each other and the omissions come
// up out loud. So it is the lab's, not the tool's.
//
// The drawing, which is the whole interaction.
//
// One question opens it: what time did you wake up. That time is printed in the
// gutter with a line drawn across the sheet beside it, and the reader pulls
// that line downward. The pull opens a block: a second line lands where the
// pointer stopped, the distance between the two lines is how long the block
// lasted, and the space between them is where you write what you were doing.
// Then you pull the new line, and the next, until you press the sleep button,
// which closes the day.
//
// Duration is a physical distance here, at one pixel to the minute, which is
// the part a list of typed times cannot do: a day drawn this way shows at a
// glance that the afternoon was three blocks and the morning was nine, and it
// shows the shape of the hours rather than their names. The space between the
// lines is also the space you write in, so a three hour block offers three
// hours of room and a twenty minute one offers a line: the field tells you how
// much of the day you are describing before you have written a word of it.
//
// Where the words go, which is a rule and not a preference. Everything that
// tells a reader how to fill the sheet sits in one block above the drawing and
// is met once, before they start: what the drawing is, the keyboard route
// through it, and what the one tick column means. The sheet itself carries no
// prose at all. Short hints beside a control were allowed and two were taken,
// one under the timeline buttons and one under the export; the author has had
// both out, on the grounds that a control whose label reads "Open the next
// block" or "Save it as a PDF" has said its piece. What is left under the
// drawing is derived rather than explanatory: three cells of figures, and the
// promise that none of this is kept, which is not an instruction but the
// reason the export exists. A line admitting that a block under half an hour
// is drawn taller than it ran was there too, and the author has had it out as
// the instrument talking about itself.
//
// Three things had to be solved before any of that was worth building.
//
// Dragging is never the only way, because WCAG 2.2 success criterion 2.5.7
// requires a single-pointer path that is not a drag, and because a keyboard
// user cannot drag at all. Every boundary is a real time input: tab to it, type
// 07:43, or hold the arrow keys, which move a minute on the minutes and an hour
// on the hours. Enter walks the two fields that carry the day and opens the
// next block at the end, so a whole day can be typed without the pointer ever
// entering the timeline, and for many readers that is the faster path rather
// than the consolation one. A tap on the grip, with no drag at all, opens one
// block of half an hour. The button beside it does the same thing and is what a
// screen reader meets, since the grip is pointer sugar and is hidden from the
// accessibility tree.
//
// Nothing rounds the drag. A pixel is a minute and the block is as long as the
// pull was, which is the second version of this: it snapped to a resolution
// select, on the reasoning that free dragging produces 07:43 and annoys people.
// It does produce 07:43, and that is the point. This lab asks for the day as it
// actually was, and 07:43 is a truer record than 07:45 rounded off for the
// instrument's convenience; the lab's fifteen minutes is plenty is about how
// finely a student needs to write, not about how precisely the tool is allowed
// to let them. The readout under the pointer names the exact time and length
// while you are still holding it, and the time inputs lost their quarter hour
// step for the same reason: a sheet that can draw 07:43 must not refuse to have
// it typed.
//
// The header is one field, the date. A resolution select and three radios for
// whether the day was written live or reconstructed were there and are not any
// more. The select had nothing left to set once the drag went continuous. The
// radios asked a student to certify that they had followed the instruction in
// the lesson above, which is a question that invites a recorded no rather than
// a changed behaviour, and the lab reads the days back to each other in the
// room where that comes up anyway. The cost is on the record: the printed sheet
// no longer says whether the day was written as it happened, and that is a real
// loss, taken deliberately.
//
// Touch and page scrolling share the same vertical gesture, so they are
// separated by area rather than by timing: touch-action: none is set on the
// grip alone, which is the only element in the component that takes a drag.
// Everywhere else on the timeline the page scrolls as it always did. A drag
// that reaches the top or bottom of the window scrolls the page itself, and the
// pull is measured in page coordinates rather than client ones, so scrolling
// under a still finger goes on lengthening the block exactly as moving the
// finger would.
//
// The sleep button is what makes the record twenty-four hours rather than a
// waking day. Pulled blocks only ever cover the hours you were awake, and the
// lab's first spec line asks for twenty-four continuous hours, so pressing
// sleep takes everything between the last line and the wake time on the
// following morning and records it as one sleep block. Nothing is left as a
// gap: the sheet says 24 h, the printed record carries the sleep row with its
// own start and length, and the whole thing closes on the line it opened on.
//
// What it does not do is store anything. There is no network call, no
// localStorage, no sessionStorage, no cookie and deliberately no <form>
// element, because a form with no action submits to the current URL and would
// put a student's day in their browser history as a query string. The printed
// file is the only copy. The course's own policy page promises that the records
// a student makes are theirs, and a page on the same site that quietly kept a
// copy would make that promise a lie.
//
// PDF export is window.print() and a print stylesheet, on purpose. This site
// has never shipped a client library and a PDF library would be the largest
// dependency in the repo, for a job the browser already does under Save as PDF.
// What print gets instead is a real layout: the script keeps a plain printable
// sheet in sync with the fields, and @media print hides the site chrome, the
// lesson and the timeline itself, so the file is a record rather than a
// screenshot of a web page.
//
// With no JavaScript a drag interface is nothing at all, so the blank sheet at
// the foot matters more here than it did in the typed version. It is ordinary
// markup, it prints as a ruled template, and the script hides it once it has
// run. Paper is what a reader with scripts off, or with the script failing,
// takes into the lab, and the lab already blesses paper.
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";

// Seven in the morning is a visible, editable first answer, not a claim about
// when anyone gets up. It is the only value in the component that starts
// filled, because the drawing needs a line to start from.
const DEFAULT_WAKE = "07:00";

// Fourteen ruled rows on the paper sheet. A day at the resolution this lab asks
// for runs to ten or a dozen blocks once sleep is one of them, and fourteen
// fills a printed page without running onto a second one.
const PAPER_ROWS = 14;
---

<section class="recorder" data-day-recorder aria-labelledby="recorder-title">
  <h3 id="recorder-title">The day sheet</h3>

  <p class="recorder-lead">
    Twenty-four hours, drawn, and what you might change. Fill it in as the day goes. It saves
    nothing.
  </p>

  <div class="recorder-live" data-live>
    <div class="recorder-how">
      <h4>How to fill it in</h4>

      <p class="recorder-help">
        Say when you woke up, then pull the line down to open a block: where you let go is when
        it ended, and the space between the lines is where you write what you were doing. A pixel
        is a minute. Keep going until you went to bed, then press the sleep button, which records
        the rest as sleep and closes the twenty-four hours. Nothing has to be dragged: every time
        here is a field you can type into, and the button under the line opens a half hour block,
        which you then correct by typing its end time on the line below it.
      </p>

      <details class="recorder-keys">
        <summary>Keyboard</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>Enter walks the start time, then what you were doing, then opens the next block.</li>
          <li>Shift with Enter starts a new line inside one.</li>
          <li>The arrows on a time move it a minute, or an hour on the hours.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <p class="recorder-help">
        Tick <b>change</b> on a block you would be willing to run differently and could actually
        reach. That is narrower than a block you dislike: the hour you resent is often the one
        somebody else's timetable fixed, and the one you could actually move is duller. The tick
        changes nothing about today. Week 1 is a baseline, and a baseline you have begun improving
        is not one, so the block goes down as it happened. Weeks 5 to 7 are the course's Change
        move, where you choose one thing and alter it. The tick is a note to yourself then, not a
        plan now.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="recorder-work">
      <div class="sheet-head">
        <h4>The sheet</h4>

        <label class="day-date">
          <span class="field-name">Date</span>
          <input type="date" data-field="date" />
        </label>
      </div>

      <div class="tl" data-timeline>
        <ol class="tl-blocks" data-blocks></ol>

        <div class="tl-row tl-end is-wake" data-end>
          <div class="tl-gutter">
            <label class="tl-time">
              <span class="field-name" data-end-label>Woke at</span>
              <input type="time" value={DEFAULT_WAKE} data-end-time />
            </label>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-body">
            <div class="tl-grip" data-grip aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl-grip-bar"></span>
              <span class="tl-readout" data-readout></span>
            </div>
            <p class="tl-actions">
              <button type="button" data-action="open">Open the next block</button>
              <button type="button" data-action="sleep">Asleep from here</button>
              <button type="button" data-action="reopen" hidden>Reopen the day</button>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tally" data-total>
        <div class="tally-figures" data-tally-figures hidden>
          <div class="tally-row">
            <div class="tally-cell tally-day" data-tally-day>
              <span class="tally-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <p class="tally-face">
                <span class="tally-line"><span class="tally-value" data-tally-covered></span></span>
                <span class="tally-label">of 24 h</span>
              </p>
              <p class="tally-face tally-face-over" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="tally-line"><span class="tally-value" data-tally-covered></span></span>
                <span class="tally-label">of 24 h</span>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="tally-cell tally-drawn">
              <p class="tally-face">
                <span class="tally-line"><span class="tally-count" data-tally-blocks></span></span>
                <span class="tally-label" data-tally-blocks-label></span>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="tally-cell tally-ticked">
              <p class="tally-face">
                <span class="tally-line"><span class="tally-count" data-tally-marked></span></span>
                <span class="tally-label">ticked as change</span>
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="recorder-tools">
        <button type="button" class="print-button" data-action="print">
          <span data-print-word>Save it as a PDF</span><span
            class="recorder-hidden"
            data-print-tail></span>
        </button>
      </p>

      <p class="recorder-privacy">
        Nothing here is saved. This page sends nothing anywhere, writes nothing to this browser, and
        loses the lot when the tab is closed or reloaded. The file you save is the only copy and it
        is yours, which is what the course's policy on{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/policies/#the-records-you-make-are-yours")}>the records you make</a> says
        too.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="recorder-status" role="status" data-status></p>
  </div>

  <div class="recorder-paper" data-paper>
    <h4>The same sheet, to fill in by hand</h4>
    <p class="recorder-help">
      If the line above will not pull and the buttons do nothing, scripts are not running here.
      Print the page and write the day in with a pen: the lab counts that identically, and the
      ruled sheet below is what prints.
    </p>

    <p class="paper-date">Date</p>

    <table class="paper-table">
      <caption>One day, in blocks. A block runs until the next one starts.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Starts</th>
          <th scope="col">What you were doing</th>
          <th scope="col">Might change</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          Array.from({ length: PAPER_ROWS }, (_, index) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><span class="recorder-hidden">Block {index + 1}</span></th>
              <td></td>
              <td></td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="recorder-sheet" data-sheet></div>

  <template data-block-template>
    <li class="tl-row tl-block" data-block data-mins="60">
      <div class="tl-gutter">
        <label class="tl-time">
          <span class="field-name" data-time-label>Block starts at</span>
          <input type="time" data-time />
        </label>
      </div>
      <label class="tl-what">
        <span class="field-name">What you were doing</span>
        <textarea rows="1" data-what autocomplete="off"></textarea>
      </label>
      <p class="tl-controls">
        <label class="tl-flag">
          <input type="checkbox" data-flag />
          <span
            ><span class="recorder-hidden">Mark this block as one you might </span><span
              class="tl-flag-word">change</span
            ><span class="recorder-hidden"> in weeks 5 to 7</span></span
          >
        </label>
        <button type="button" class="tl-remove" data-action="remove">
          <span aria-hidden="true">&#215;</span><span class="recorder-hidden">Remove this block</span>
        </button>
      </p>
    </li>
  </template>
</section>

<style>
  /* The outline is the component, and it is deliberate. Do not remove it.
   *
   * The block sits in the lesson, so it is outlined rather than filled: the
   * only tinted box on a lab page is the safety block, and a second one would
   * compete with it. Border and radius are the theme's, so light and dark
   * arrive without a second palette.
   *
   * This has now nearly been deleted twice, by two agents reading the same
   * standing instruction, so the reading is written down here. "Remove the
   * rounded corner block, so the entire region could fill with actual content,
   * just like a table" was about an inner block that used to sit inside the
   * sheet, not about this container. The settled intent: each component on the
   * page is a box, and the shared visual language lives inside the box. This
   * border is what says where the day sheet begins and ends, and it is what
   * separates the sheet from the component under it, so neither of them has to
   * hold space open on the other's behalf.
   *
   * It was taken off for one build and put back unchanged. Flush against the
   * page column the sheet did fill its width like a table, and it also stopped
   * being a thing: the drawing, the figures and the promise read as three
   * pieces of the lesson that happened to be adjacent. */
  .recorder {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    padding: var(--at-spacing-lg);
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
  }

  .recorder h3 {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-xs);
  }

  .recorder h4 {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl) var(--at-spacing-xs);
  }

  .recorder h4:first-child {
    margin-block-start: 0;
  }

  .recorder-lead {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-lg);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  /* Two zones, and the gap between them is the whole mechanism.
   *
   * The component used to be prose with a drawing in the middle of it: an
   * instruction, a field, another instruction, the sheet, a paragraph about
   * the tick, then the sheet's figures. A reader could not tell where the
   * reading stopped and the doing started, because it stopped and started four
   * times. Now it is how to fill it in, all of it, once; then the sheet, with
   * everything that acts on the sheet, including the button that takes it
   * away.
   *
   * Drawn with a hairline, a heading and a great deal of air, and emphatically
   * not with a second bordered box. The author has asked for the rounded
   * corner block to go so the region fills its width like a table, and
   * answering "separate these two" with a card would walk that straight back.
   *
   * Sixty-four pixels above the rule and thirty-two below it. The largest gap
   * anywhere inside either zone is the thirty-two an h4 sets, so the boundary
   * is three times the biggest interior gap and reads as a boundary without
   * anything being drawn heavily. The rule is --at-border rather than the
   * fainter --at-divider the figures use, so the two hairlines in the
   * component rank: one separates the zones, one separates a readout from the
   * drawing it describes. */
  .recorder-work {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-2xl);
    padding-block-start: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  /* Instructions, not prose: one step smaller and quieter than the lesson
     around them, and never carrying a fact that is not also in a control. */
  .recorder-help {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xs) var(--at-spacing-md);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  /* The sheet's masthead: the zone heading on the left, the date in the
   * corner, one line, with the sheet's own first rule under both.
   *
   * The date was a form row for several versions, a stacked uppercase label
   * over a field on a line of its own under the heading, and it read as the
   * orphan of a form rather than as a heading: four other fields were cut from
   * this header earlier in the project and the one that stayed kept their
   * layout, so the empty space beside it looked like something missing. It is
   * not a form row. It is the one piece of metadata the sheet carries, and a
   * day sheet on paper puts the date in the top corner, which is also where
   * somebody filling in a printed form goes looking for it.
   *
   * Baseline alignment rather than centres, so the date sits on the heading's
   * own line rather than beside its box, and it wraps to its own line under
   * the heading when there is not room for both, which on the narrowest phone
   * there is not. */
  .sheet-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-xs) var(--at-spacing-md);
  }

  .recorder .sheet-head h4 {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Keyboard help, behind a native disclosure.
   *
   * <details> and <summary> rather than a button and a hidden div: it is
   * keyboard operable, it is announced as expandable, and it opens and closes
   * without a line of script, so there is nothing here to rebind on
   * astro:page-load and nothing that breaks with JavaScript off. Closed by
   * default because the shortcuts are the fast path for a reader who already
   * knows the sheet, not five lines to meet on arrival.
   *
   * It sat beside the buttons in the end row for one version, on the reading
   * that a disclosure is a hint attached to a control. It is not. What is
   * behind it is the typing route through the whole day, Enter walking time to
   * activity to the next block, which is an instruction for filling the sheet
   * and belongs with the others in front of it. The end row is also the worst
   * possible shelf: it is the live edge of the drawing and it moves down the
   * page every time a block opens, so a reader who wanted the shortcuts twice
   * had to go looking for them.
   *
   * It never prints, and needs no rule of its own to make that true: the print
   * stylesheet below hides the whole of .recorder-live. That is the right
   * answer as well as the free one. The printed sheet is filled in with a pen,
   * and keyboard shortcuts on paper are ink somebody paid for, describing a
   * control they are not holding. */
  .recorder-keys {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  /* fit-content rather than display: inline-block, which would take the
     disclosure triangle with it: the marker is drawn because summary is a
     list-item, and changing its display removes the marker in Chrome. */
  .recorder-keys > summary {
    width: fit-content;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .recorder-keys > summary:hover {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    color: var(--at-text);
  }

  .recorder-keys ul {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xs) 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.2rem;
  }

  .recorder-keys li {
    margin-block-end: 0.15rem;
  }

  .field-name {
    display: block;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* The clocks.
   *
   * They stay native <input type="time">. Typing, the arrow keys, the
   * reader's own twelve or twenty-four hour presentation, the phone's picker
   * and a screen reader's field by field announcement all arrive with the
   * element, and every one of them would have to be rebuilt, badly, on a div
   * holding two number boxes. So everything here is paint on a native control
   * and nothing else: no property below changes what the control does.
   *
   * Tabular figures because these are read as a column down the sheet rather
   * than as single values. Proportional digits make a one narrower than a
   * zero, so a day of blocks ticking over jitters sideways while you watch it.
   * font-variant-numeric comes after `font: inherit` on purpose: the font
   * shorthand resets the variant, so the order is what makes it stick. */
  .recorder input[type="time"],
  .recorder input[type="date"] {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.45rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .recorder :is(input[type="time"], input[type="date"]):hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 50%, var(--at-border));
  }

  /* Focus on two channels: the ring the whole component shares, and the
     field's own edge and fill. The ring is what clears the 3:1 an indicator
     owes; the tint is what makes the field you are in findable in a column of
     a dozen identical ones. Neither changes a border width or a padding, so
     nothing on the sheet moves when focus lands. */
  .recorder :is(input[type="time"], input[type="date"]):focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 8%, var(--at-bg));
  }

  /* The clock glyph. Chrome and Safari draw it, Firefox draws nothing at all
     and never has, which is the rule for this pseudo element: it may be
     painted and it may not be leaned on. Everything it opens is reachable by
     typing and by the arrows, so a browser that ignores every line below
     loses an affordance and no capability. Dimmed to about the weight of
     secondary text so it stops out-inking the value beside it, and full
     strength under the pointer or while the field holds focus. */
  .recorder :is(input[type="time"], input[type="date"])::-webkit-calendar-picker-indicator {
    margin-inline-start: 0.3rem;
    padding: 0;
    opacity: 0.45;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .recorder
    :is(input[type="time"], input[type="date"]):is(:hover, :focus-within)::-webkit-calendar-picker-indicator {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* The checkboxes and radios take the brand gold, which is the one place a
     colour is allowed to carry a state here, and never the only place: the
     control's own tick is the channel that does the work. */
  .recorder input[type="checkbox"] {
    accent-color: var(--at-primary);
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 1.1rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Stated rather than inherited. The theme's focus ring is set on its own
     components, and every control in here is an unstyled element the theme
     never sees; a form this size is exactly where a missing ring strands a
     keyboard user. Gold against either page colour clears the 3:1 that a
     non-text indicator owes. */
  .recorder :is(input, textarea, button, a, summary):focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--at-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .recorder button {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .recorder button:hover {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  /* The export is the only action on the sheet that produces something, so it
     is the only one with the brand on it. The gold is the border and not the
     text: at 3.5:1 against the page it is sound as an edge and short of the
     4.5:1 that the words inside it owe a reader.

     Two states, because the button says two things now. Its label carries the
     empty sheet, so the brand arrives with the first block: the sheet becomes
     worth saving and the control that saves it becomes the action.

     The first version of this moved the border colour alone, from --at-border
     to the gold, and the author could not see it. They were right and the
     numbers agreed once the right two things were compared. Each edge against
     the page reads 1.4:1 and 3.4:1, which sounds like a difference, but the
     eye is comparing the two states with each other and not either with the
     page: the two edges are the same hue at two alphas and come to 2.3:1
     against one another, across about one and a half rendered pixels, with an
     identical fill behind them. A hairline changing saturation is not a state
     change. So the fill moves now as well, from the ordinary button surface to
     the brand, which is 4.1:1 between the two states in light and 3.6:1 in
     dark and cannot be missed.

     What holds from the first version: the box does not move. Same width, same
     padding, same border width, same focus ring, so nothing shifts when the
     first block opens. And the quiet state must not read as disabled, because
     pressing it is the route to a sheet on paper and a student who wants paper
     presses it before recording anything. It is an ordinary button of this
     component, the same fill and edge and full strength ink as every other
     one, and nothing about it is dimmed.

     The ink flips with the fill, to --at-on-primary, which is the theme's own
     answer to what survives on a brand surface and lands at 5.6:1 with this
     gold. It clears 4.5:1 in both states in both schemes, so the label is never
     the thing that pays for the emphasis.

     This is now a filled brand button, which the Open control is not: that one
     takes a twelve percent wash and a one pixel border, and the distinction
     between the repeated action and the one that produces a file survives at a
     glance. Colour is still the second channel and never the only one. The
     label is the first, and it is what a forced colours reader and a printed
     page get, where neither a border colour nor a fill is kept. The default in
     the markup is the emphatic state, so a reader with no script, whose button
     prints the ruled blank sheet, gets it rather than the state a script would
     have set. */
  .recorder .print-button {
    width: 100%;
    padding-block: 0.45rem;
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    border-width: 2px;
    background: var(--at-primary);
    color: var(--at-on-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .recorder .print-button:hover {
    border-color: var(--at-primary-hover);
    background: var(--at-primary-hover);
  }

  .recorder .print-button[data-ready="false"] {
    border-color: var(--at-border);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    color: var(--at-text);
  }

  .recorder .print-button[data-ready="false"]:hover {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  /* The label rides beside the field instead of above it, because the stack
     was what made one field look like the top of a form, and the field is
     sized by its own content rather than stretched: a date input asks for
     exactly the room dd/mm/yyyy and its picker glyph need, which is why the
     width: 100% every other control in here takes is turned off for this one.
     Ragged right edges against the wake time below were part of what looked
     untidy, and this ends them by not aligning to that edge at all: the date
     hangs off the right of the sheet, the clocks stay in the left gutter. */
  .day-date {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.45rem;
  }

  .day-date .field-name {
    display: inline;
  }

  .recorder .day-date input {
    width: auto;
  }

  /* The timeline.
   *
   * One pixel to the minute, so an hour is sixty pixels and a drag is the
   * length of the thing it describes. Every row is the same two columns: the
   * time in the gutter and the sheet beside it, with the row's top border
   * drawn across both as the line the reader pulls.
   *
   * A block's height is its duration, with a floor of 2.25rem. The floor only
   * bites below half an hour: it exists because a fifteen minute block is 15px
   * and a time field is 22, and a row shorter than its own contents is not a
   * row. Half an hour is also the length of an undragged block, so everything
   * the buttons make is drawn exactly. */
  .tl {
    --px-per-min: 1px;
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-md) var(--at-spacing-sm);
  }

  .tl-blocks {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .tl-row {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0 var(--at-spacing-xs);
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  /* The floor, and what it costs.
   *
   * A block is its duration in pixels, so above half an hour the drawing is
   * exact: a three hour block is 180px and holds three hours of writing. Below
   * it, a fifteen minute block would be fifteen pixels, which cannot hold a
   * word or a time field, so everything under thirty minutes is drawn thirty
   * tall. Thirty was chosen because it is the roundest number that fits the
   * contents, so the rule is "half an hour and over is to scale".
   *
   * A line under the drawing used to say so whenever it was true of a block,
   * and the author has had it out: a minimum mark size is the instrument's
   * business and not something a student can act on. What that leaves is a
   * drawing that is approximate for short blocks and a record that is not,
   * which is the ordinary arrangement for any drawing with a minimum mark. The
   * times are the record and they stay on the page: a floored block shows its
   * own start in the gutter and the line below it shows where it ended, which
   * is how every block on this sheet is read, and the printed record states
   * each length as a number with no drawing in it at all. Checked on a twelve
   * minute block rather than assumed: the row is 30px, both times render, and
   * so do the writing field and the tick. */
  .tl-block {
    height: max(30px, calc(var(--mins, 60) * var(--px-per-min)));
  }

  /* Flush with the line, which is the whole point of the column.
   *
   * Each of these times names the boundary at the top of its own row, and the
   * gutter used to hold them 4.3px under it while the writing area beside them
   * started 0.7px under it. Two things on one row, starting on two different
   * lines, in the one component where a horizontal line is a time. They start
   * together now.
   *
   * It also buys back the room the wash needed. A default block is thirty
   * minutes and thirty pixels, the field is 25.1 of them, and at the old offset
   * it finished 1.2px past the next boundary: with no border to see, that was
   * invisible, and with a tint behind the digits it would have been a block of
   * colour crossing a line that means something. */
  .tl-gutter {
    align-self: start;
    padding-block-start: 0;
  }

  /* The vertical rule down the sheet, which is what makes the horizontal lines
     read as a timeline rather than as a stack of form rows. */
  .tl-body {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-xs);
    padding: 0.25rem 0 0 var(--at-spacing-sm);
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  /* The theme gives every label a bottom margin of --at-spacing-xs, which is
     four pixels of somebody else's spacing inside a row whose whole height is
     the duration: it pushed the gutter to 35px inside a 30px block and left
     the writing area four pixels short of the line below it. */
  .recorder :is(.tl-time, .tl-what) {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }

  .tl-time {
    display: block;
  }

  /* The gutter, which is the column a reader looks at more than anything else
     here.
     *
     * It was a stack of bordered rounded boxes: every time in its own little
     * card, floating beside a drawing that is one continuous timeline. The
     * boxes belonged to a form and the column belongs to a sheet. A paper day
     * sheet writes its times in the margin, so these are set as text in the
     * margin, and the field appearance arrives on hover and on focus instead of
     * sitting there all day.
     *
     * The border is kept and made transparent rather than removed, and the
     * radius is zeroed rather than the box being changed, so the border box is
     * identical in all three states. Nothing on the drawing moves when a
     * pointer crosses the column or when focus lands, which matters more here
     * than anywhere else in the component: every one of these fields sits on a
     * boundary line, and a field that grew by a pixel would drag the line it
     * names.
     *
     * What a reader who has not hovered anything has to go on, since this is
     * the risk in taking chrome off a control. Two things. The gutter is
     * already a ruled column: every row draws its boundary across the gutter as
     * well as the drawing, and the sheet's vertical rule closes it on the
     * right, so each time already sits in a ruled cell of a form, which is
     * where a person writes. On top of that a wash of the brand stays behind
     * the digits at rest, which says "field" the way a filled text input does,
     * without a border, without a corner, and without adding a horizontal line
     * to a drawing whose horizontal lines all mean something.
     *
     * The wash is 22% and the number was chosen by measuring, not by feel,
     * because the mistake this component just made twice is a change that is
     * real in the stylesheet and invisible on the page. --at-accent-soft, the
     * theme's token for exactly this job, is 10% and comes to 1.11:1 against
     * the page, which is about a third of a table stripe and not an
     * affordance. 22% reads 1.28:1 in light and 1.27:1 in dark, near enough the
     * same step in both schemes, with the digits still at 14:1 on top of it.
     *
     * Which is why the obvious treatment, a rule under the time the way a paper
     * form gives you a line to write on, is not what this uses. Measured rather
     * than argued: a default block is thirty minutes, which is the drawing's
     * floor, so the commonest row on the sheet is thirty pixels tall and the
     * field sits twenty of them. An underline would land six pixels above the
     * next boundary, and on a sheet where a horizontal line means a time, a
     * second line six pixels from a real one is not a rule to write on, it is a
     * misreading waiting to happen.
     *
     * The column is uniform now, which it was not. Every time in it is the same
     * size, and none of them carries a picker glyph. The glyph was on the end
     * row and not on the blocks, for a reason a reader has no way of knowing:
     * three fields in one column wearing two treatments reads as an oversight.
     * Hiding it costs nothing anywhere: Firefox never drew it, a phone opens
     * its own picker on a tap on the field itself, and the arrows and the
     * keyboard reach every minute of the day either way. The date up in the
     * masthead keeps its glyph, because that one is a form field in a header
     * and not a mark in a margin. */
  .recorder .tl-time input {
    padding: 0 0.2rem;
    border-color: transparent;
    border-radius: 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 22%, transparent);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
  }

  .recorder .tl-time input::-webkit-calendar-picker-indicator {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The edge arrives on the way in, and the wash deepens under focus. Colour
     only, in every state: the border width, the radius, the padding and the
     font never move, so a pointer crossing the column cannot nudge a line that
     names a time. The corners stay square throughout, because a rounded field
     appearing under the pointer is the card coming back.

     Both of these override the component's shared field rules, which lighten a
     time input towards the page colour on hover and focus. That is right for
     the date up in the masthead, which rests on the page; it is backwards here,
     where the resting state is already a wash and lightening it would read as
     the field going away as you reach for it. The progression runs one way:
     22% at rest, an edge on hover, 32% and the gold under focus. */
  .recorder .tl-time input:hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 60%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 22%, transparent);
  }

  .recorder .tl-time input:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    border-radius: 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 32%, transparent);
  }

  /* Drawn tight to the field. Two pixels out would put the ring on the line
     above the block, and that line is a boundary in the drawing rather than
     decoration. */
  .recorder .tl-block .tl-time input:focus-visible {
    outline-offset: 1px;
  }

  /* One exception to the uniform column, and it is the state where there is no
     column: an empty sheet, where the end row is the only field on the drawing
     and is the question the sheet opens with. There it takes the ordinary field
     size and keeps its spelled out label. The moment a block exists it joins
     the column and is set like the rest of it. */
  .recorder .tl-end.is-wake .tl-time input {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.4rem;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  /* Clipped on every row but the first. The gutter is a column of times under
     a line each, which says what they are; the wake time is the question the
     sheet opens with and keeps its label. */
  .tl-block .field-name,
  .tl-end .field-name {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The opening question, visible only while the sheet is empty.
   *
   * Two things had to be true at once: the first thing a reader meets should
   * say "Woke at" in words, and every row of a drawn day has to fit inside its
   * own duration, which at the floor is thirty pixels and does not have room
   * for a label above a field. The empty state is the end row, which has no
   * duration at all, so the label lives there and is clipped everywhere else.
   * The accessible name never changes: the clipped label is the same words.
   *
   * It was .tl-row.is-wake .field-name for one build, which also unclipped the
   * writing area's label on the first block and pushed a visible WHAT YOU WERE
   * DOING above a field sized to the block. */
  .tl-end.is-wake .tl-gutter .field-name {
    position: static;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    clip-path: none;
  }

  /* The block is the writing area. It fills the space between the two lines
     edge to edge, because that space is the duration and the room to write in
     ought to be the same thing.
     A textarea rather than an input: an input centres its text in the box, so
     in a three hour block the words floated in the middle of the space instead
     of starting under the line, and a tall block is exactly where somebody
     wants a second line anyway. Enter belongs to the sheet, so Shift and Enter
     is the newline. */
  .tl-what {
    position: relative;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Absolutely positioned inside the block, which is the only way the height
     can be the duration and nothing else. The theme gives every textarea a
     min-height of 6rem, so in flow a fifteen minute block came out 108px tall
     and the grid row grew to fit it: the drawing was being sized by its
     contents rather than by the day. Out of flow it cannot push, and inset: 0
     is what makes it fill the space between the two lines edge to edge. */
  .recorder .tl-what textarea {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    min-height: 0;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.4rem;
    border: 0;
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-border) 10%, transparent);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    line-height: 1.4;
    resize: none;
  }

  /* What is left of the affordance once the box is gone. The tint says the
     region takes writing, the way a filled cell in a table does; it deepens
     under the pointer, and again while it holds focus. */
  .recorder .tl-what textarea:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-border) 20%, transparent);
  }

  /* The focus ring is the whole cell. Drawn inside the edge, because an
     outline offset outwards would sit on the line above and on the block
     below; 2px of the brand gold against either page colour clears the 3:1 a
     focus indicator owes, and it marks a much larger shape than the old ring
     around a small box did. The tint changes with it, so focus is not carried
     by the outline alone. */
  .recorder .tl-what textarea:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--at-primary);
    outline-offset: -2px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 8%, transparent);
  }

  /* Beside the writing area rather than inside it: an overlay would either
     cover the first line of the text or reserve a column of padding on every
     line of it. Side by side rather than stacked, because a thirty pixel block
     is one row tall and two of these are 46px wide against 42px of height. */
  .tl-controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    align-self: start;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding-block-start: 0.2rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Below 30rem the word goes and the checkbox stays, because the activity
     field is the thing that has to be typeable and 81px is not a field. The
     word is only ever clipped, never removed, so the control keeps the same
     accessible name at every width: "Mark this block as one you might change
     in weeks 5 to 7". */
  @media (width < 30rem) {
    .tl-flag-word {
      position: absolute;
      width: 1px;
      height: 1px;
      overflow: hidden;
      clip-path: inset(50%);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }

  .tl-flag {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
  }

  .recorder .tl-flag input[type="checkbox"] {
    width: 0.95rem;
    height: 0.95rem;
  }

  .recorder .tl-remove {
    flex: none;
    padding: 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* A marked block takes the gold on its own rule, and the checkbox beside it
     stays ticked: two channels, so the mark survives a greyscale print and a
     reader who cannot separate the gold from the border grey. */
  .recorder .tl-block.is-marked .tl-what textarea {
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
  }

  .recorder .tl-block[data-sleep] .tl-what textarea {
    border-inline-start-style: dashed;
  }

  /* The grip: the only element in the component that takes a drag, and so the
     only one that turns off the browser's own touch scrolling. Everywhere else
     on the timeline a finger scrolls the page as usual. The hit area is 44px
     tall for a target that a thumb can find; the bar inside it is what is
     drawn. */
  /* Its own full line in the end row, and the buttons take the line under it.
     Sizing it as an ordinary flex item put the three buttons beside it at
     max-content, which at 390px pushed the whole page 103px wider than the
     window: the timeline fitted and the row under it did not. */
  .tl-grip {
    position: relative;
    flex: 1 1 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    padding-block: 1.1rem;
    cursor: grab;
    touch-action: none;
  }

  .tl-grip:active {
    cursor: grabbing;
  }

  .tl-grip-bar {
    display: block;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 55%, transparent);
  }

  .tl-grip:hover .tl-grip-bar,
  .tl.is-dragging .tl-grip-bar {
    background: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .tl-readout {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 0;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    color: var(--at-text);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* The two buttons take a line and divide it, rather than sitting at their
     own widths with the rest of the line empty beside them.
   *
   * auto-fit is doing real work here and not decoration. Three buttons live in
   * this row and only ever two of them are in the document at once: while the
   * day is open it is Open and Asleep, and once it closes both are hidden and
   * Reopen takes their place. With fixed halves that last state would leave a
   * half width button stranded beside a gap. auto-fit collapses the empty
   * track, so one visible button fills the line and two split it, with nothing
   * in the markup or the script having to know which case it is in.
   *
   * The min() in the track floor is the guard against the narrow case: a bare
   * minmax(11rem, 1fr) would refuse to shrink below 11rem and push the row
   * past the window at 390px. With min(11rem, 100%) the track gives way, the
   * second button drops to a line of its own, and each one is still the full
   * width of the row. */
  .tl-actions {
    display: grid;
    flex: 1 1 100%;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(11rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    min-width: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Full width targets, so a little more height to go with the width: about
     43px against the 24 that WCAG 2.5.8 asks for, and eight pixels of gap
     between the two halves so a thumb cannot land on both edges at once.
   *
   * The inline padding comes in rather than out, which is the opposite of what
   * a bigger button usually wants. At 390px the row is 167px wide and "Open
   * the next block" needs 148 of it, so the default 0.7rem either side broke
   * the label across two lines and made one button half again as tall as the
   * other. A centred label in a full width button has all the air it needs
   * from the width; the padding was only ever sizing the box. */
  .recorder .tl-actions button {
    padding: 0.45rem 0.4rem;
  }

  /* Same width, different weight. These two are not equal actions: Open is
     pressed a dozen times in a day and Asleep once, at the end of it, so the
     repeated one reads as the default. It takes weight and a wash of the brand
     rather than a heavier border, which is the export button's mark and stays
     the export button's: this one is the default step, that one is the thing
     that produces a file. Asleep is left exactly as it was rather than being
     dimmed, because it is not a lesser action, it is a rarer one, and a
     control that looks disabled is a control people do not press when they
     need it. */
  .recorder .tl-actions button[data-action="open"] {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 12%, var(--at-bg-alt));
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .tl-end .tl-body {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    grid-column: 2 / -1;
    border-inline-start-color: transparent;
  }

  /* The figures under the sheet: three bounded cells, and nothing else.
   *
   * Three things are counted here and the author asked for three cells with an
   * edge around each, so the row is a row and not a paragraph of numbers.
   * Coverage of the twenty-four hours leads because it is the week's own spec
   * line and because it is the only figure here that cannot be gamed:
   * splitting a block in two changes the count and leaves the coverage exactly
   * where it was. The other two are plain facts. Eleven blocks is not better
   * than six, and a sheet that implied otherwise would buy itself a more
   * eventful day, which is the one failure week 1 warns about and week 4
   * writes a stopping rule for. So neither of them gets a meter, a percentage
   * or a target, and the third is the quietest thing in the row.
   *
   * Three cells and no prose. A state pill and a sentence naming the
   * unaccounted stretch both stood here and the author has taken them out. The
   * pill said in a small word what the sheet already says loudly: the buttons
   * swap, the grip goes, the end row relabels to "Back to waking at" and
   * locks, and a sleep block appears with the word in it. The sentence read
   * "18 h 52 min unaccounted for, from 12:08 back around to 07:00", and both
   * of its times are on the drawing already, since the blocks run contiguously
   * from the wake line down and the gap is always the tail. What it added was
   * the subtraction, and the subtraction is the cell beside it read the other
   * way round. Nothing explanatory sits under the drawing now, which is the
   * rule the zones were built on.
   *
   * Flat and table like, which is the author's standing instruction about this
   * region holding even though a boundary has now been asked for. One hairline
   * around the row and one between the cells, drawn as a one pixel grid gap
   * with the border colour behind it, so every edge is shared and none of them
   * doubles up whatever way the row wraps. No radius and no shadow: three
   * floating panels would be a dashboard, and this is a readout on a sheet.
   *
   * Not a live region, deliberately. layout() runs on every keystroke in a
   * time field, so a polite region here would recite the coverage after every
   * arrow press. The settled outcome of each action is already announced by
   * .recorder-status, which fires once per block opened, removed or closed.
   *
   * It does not print, and does not need a rule to say so: the print
   * stylesheet hides .recorder-live whole. That is the right answer as well as
   * the free one. Without the script this row never leaves its hidden
   * attribute, so what would print is three empty cells and a coverage figure
   * computed from a sheet nobody has filled in; with the script, the rebuilt
   * record carries coverage in its own facts line, in type set for paper. The
   * row is a screen readout of a sheet in progress, and a sheet on paper is
   * either finished or blank. */
  .tally {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The divider and the space sit on the figures rather than on the wrapper
     around them, because the wrapper is now empty on an untouched sheet: the
     line that used to say "Nothing recorded yet." is the export button's label
     now. On the wrapper this rule drew itself under the drawing with nothing
     beneath it, a hairline and twenty five pixels of air announcing a readout
     that was not there. */
  .tally-figures {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
    padding-block-start: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  /* The row. One cell over two by default, three across once all three fit.
     Measured rather than guessed, in a same origin iframe at each width, since
     Chrome will not make a window narrow enough to check a phone.

     At a 390px viewport this region is 295px wide. Across three that is 98px a
     cell, against a widest line of 157px in the first cell and 123px in the
     third, so the day takes the whole line and the two counts split the one
     under it. That is the better shape for the meter as well, which wants
     length. Three across arrives at the 40rem the timeline already breaks at,
     where the region is 545px.

     Neither split is even, and the numbers in it are measured contents rather
     than a feeling about importance. The widest line each cell has to hold is
     157px for a coverage figure at 1.5rem, which is "23 h 46 min" and not the
     "24 h" of a closed day, 97px for "blocks drawn" and 123px for "ticked as
     change"; with the padding that is 179, 119 and 145, or 1.5 to 1 to 1.2.
     The first cell being the widest is what the type asks for as well as what
     the headline deserves.

     Worth recording, because it was the opposite of what was expected: this
     split did not change when the state word came out of the first cell. That
     word shared its line with the unit and the two came to 138px, less than
     the figure above them, so the figure was always the binding constraint and
     still is. What the remeasure changed was the slack, which now runs 20, 14
     and 14 pixels at the tightest three across width, where it used to run 15,
     20 and 14 and the cell with the least room to spare was the one carrying
     the headline.

     The gap is the hairline: one pixel of the border colour showing between
     opaque cells, plus a border around the outside. Borders on the cells
     themselves would double at every shared edge and would need a different
     rule for each way the row wraps. */
  .tally-row {
    --tally-pad-block: 0.55rem;
    --tally-pad-inline: 0.7rem;

    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.2fr;
    gap: 1px;
    /* Clear air under the divider that closes the drawing. The divider stays,
       because it is what separates the readout from the drawing whether or not
       there is a row to draw, and because the empty sheet has no box of its
       own. Set tight, the two would read as one doubled border rather than as
       a rule and a table. */
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    background: var(--at-border);
  }

  .tally-cell {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--tally-pad-block) var(--tally-pad-inline);
    background: var(--at-bg);
  }

  .tally-day {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  @media (width >= 40rem) {
    .tally-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1.5fr 1fr 1.2fr;
    }

    .tally-day {
      grid-column: auto;
    }
  }

  /* Two lines in every cell: the figure, then what it is. Kept to one type
     size across the three so the row has a single baseline and reads as a row
     rather than as three widgets. The first cell's caption line carries two
     things, the unit and the state word, and the state takes the far end of
     it: inside a bounded cell that reads as the cell's other corner rather
     than as a stray, which is what it read as when the readout had no edges to
     sit in. */
  .tally-face {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.1rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .tally-line {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 0.4em;
  }

  .tally-value,
  .tally-count {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  /* Weight and ink carry the ranking, since the size does not. Coverage is the
     one figure a student cannot improve by recording differently, so it is the
     only one set bold. The count of blocks is a fact at ordinary weight. The
     ticks are a step quieter again: same size, same weight, secondary ink and
     a muted label, because the tick is optional and a number nobody is being
     scored on should not be the brightest thing in the row. */
  .tally-value {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .tally-ticked .tally-count {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .tally-label {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .tally-ticked .tally-label {
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
  }

  /* The day laid flat, and the cell is the drawing. The reader has spent the
     last ten minutes pulling a line down a page at a pixel to the minute, and
     this is the same day wrapped into one box: empty at the start of it, full
     when the twenty-four hours are all accounted for.

     The fill is inside the padding box, so the cell's own hairline stays
     drawn at every percentage and the boundary of the cell is never the
     boundary of the fill. */
  .tally-fill {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    inline-size: var(--covered, 0%);
    background: var(--at-primary);
  }

  /* Readability across the fill edge, which is the one hard problem in this
     row.

     The figure is painted twice. The copy in the flow is --at-text on the
     cell's own surface; the copy above it is --at-on-primary, the theme's ink
     for anything sitting on a brand fill, clipped to exactly the filled
     region. Both the fill's width and the clip's edge resolve the same
     --covered against the same box, so they land on the same pixel column.
     Every glyph pixel left of that column is on-primary over primary, 5.6:1
     with this brand's gold; every pixel right of it is text over background,
     17.9:1 for the figure and 8.9:1 for the label under it. Sampled off a
     canvas rather than estimated, and the same in dark mode, where the fill
     does not change colour. No ratio depends on where the edge is, so the
     guarantee at one percent fill is the guarantee at ninety-nine.

     What this replaces is a text shadow, which passes at whatever fill level
     you happen to look at and fails at the others, and mix-blend-mode, which
     produces a contrast the author cannot state. The copy is aria-hidden so
     the figure is announced once.

     Everything in the over copy is full strength on-primary, including the
     parts that are secondary ink below. --at-on-primary is picked to clear
     4.5:1 against the fill at full opacity and nothing guarantees it at 78%,
     so on the filled side the hierarchy is carried by size and weight
     alone. */
  .tally-face-over {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    padding: var(--tally-pad-block) var(--tally-pad-inline);
    color: var(--at-on-primary);
    clip-path: inset(0 calc(100% - var(--covered, 0%)) 0 0);
  }

  .tally-face-over .tally-label {
    color: inherit;
  }

  /* Forced colours throw the fill away: a background colour is not something
     the mode keeps, and the second copy of the figure would then be system ink
     sitting exactly on top of system ink. So the copy goes and the meter falls
     back to the six pixel bar this readout used to carry, along the foot of
     the cell, drawn in the system's own text colour. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .tally-face-over {
      display: none;
    }

    .tally-fill {
      inset-block-start: auto;
      block-size: 6px;
      background: CanvasText;
    }
  }

  /* Standing alone under the button now that the hint between them has gone,
     so it takes the gap the hint used to hold open: the two margins that met
     here were both --at-spacing-xs and collapsed to four pixels, which read as
     a caption on the button rather than as the page's own promise. */
  .recorder-privacy {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-sm) var(--at-spacing-md);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .recorder-tools {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-md) var(--at-spacing-xs);
  }

  /* Announced, not shown.
   *
   * It was both for several versions, on the reasoning that everything the
   * buttons and the drag do happens somewhere other than where the pointer
   * was. The author has taken the visible half out: it was a sixth paragraph
   * of grey text under a sheet that already carries four, and it sat after
   * the drawing, where nothing explanatory is meant to sit.
   *
   * What it must not become is display: none, which would take the element out
   * of the accessibility tree and stop role="status" announcing anything at
   * all. That would leave a screen reader user with no evidence that the
   * button did something, since what it does is change a table they cannot
   * see. So it is clipped by the project's visually hidden pattern, the one
   * CertaintyOfStrong.astro uses: one pixel, positioned out, inset(50%).
   *
   * The cost is on the record and it is real. A sighted keyboard user pressing
   * Open the next block now has only the drawing to tell them what happened,
   * and the line that said "nothing was saved" after the print dialogue closed
   * is no longer read by anyone who is not using a screen reader. */
  .recorder-status {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .recorder-hidden {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  @media (width >= 40rem) {
    .tl-row {
      grid-template-columns: 7.5rem minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    }

    .tl-flag {
      font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    }
  }

  /* The blank sheet, which is what a reader without scripts gets. The script
     hides it the moment it runs, so with scripts it is neither seen nor
     printed. */
  .recorder-paper {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    padding-block-start: var(--at-spacing-md);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  .recorder[data-scripted="true"] .recorder-paper {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The same masthead, for a pen. The blank sheet is what prints when the
     script has not run, and it had a table with no date on it: the record the
     script builds carries the date in its facts line, and the one somebody
     fills in by hand had nowhere to write it. A word and a rule to write on,
     in the same border the table below it uses. */
  .paper-date {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-sm);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .paper-date::after {
    content: "";
    flex: 1;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  .paper-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .paper-table :is(th, td) {
    padding: 0.55rem 0.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    text-align: start;
  }

  .paper-table tbody th {
    width: 7rem;
  }

  .paper-table tbody td:last-child {
    width: 8rem;
  }

  /* The printed sheet is built by the script and is the only thing on the
     paper. Off screen entirely until the print stylesheet asks for it. */
  .recorder-sheet {
    display: none;
  }

  /* What prints.
   *
   * The brief for the file was that it be a record and not a screenshot of a
   * form, so everything that is not the record goes: the site's nav, search
   * and footer, the lesson around the sheet, and the timeline itself, whose
   * input boxes print as empty rectangles and whose sixty pixels an hour would
   * put a waking day across three sheets of paper. What is left is one of two
   * things. With the script running it is the sheet, rebuilt from the fields on
   * every change, so the print dialogue and Ctrl+P agree with each other
   * whether or not the browser fires beforeprint. Without it, it is the blank
   * ruled template.
   *
   * The hiding is done by structure rather than by naming the theme's classes:
   * everything in the body that is not the main region, and everything in the
   * main region that is not this component. That survives a theme upgrade
   * renaming its own furniture, and these rules only ever ship on the one page
   * that includes this component.
   *
   * Colour is the one place this file leaves the tokens, and it has to. The
   * page's text colour is a light-dark() pair, so a reader in dark mode prints
   * near-white text, which arrives as a blank sheet of paper on any printer
   * that is not also asked to flood the page with ink. Black on white, stated.
   *
   * Both zones go, and neither is a close call. The instructions are read once
   * on a screen by somebody who has not started yet, and a printed copy of
   * them is four paragraphs of ink explaining a drag to a reader holding a
   * pen. The sheet zone goes for the older reason: its input boxes print as
   * empty rectangles and its sixty pixels an hour would put a waking day
   * across three sheets. What replaces both is the record, rebuilt from the
   * fields, which carries the coverage figure in its own facts line so a
   * student who filled the sheet on screen still prints the summary. */
  @media print {
    :global(body > *:not(#main)) {
      display: none !important;
    }

    :global(#main > *:not(.recorder)) {
      display: none !important;
    }

    .recorder {
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      border: 0;
      color: #000;
      background: #fff;
    }

    .recorder-live,
    .recorder > h3,
    .recorder > .recorder-lead {
      display: none;
    }

    .recorder[data-scripted="true"] .recorder-sheet {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  @page {
    margin: 18mm;
  }

  /* The sheet itself, styled through :global because the script builds these
     elements and Astro's scope attribute only lands on markup that was in the
     template. Scoped through the wrapper, which was, so the names cannot leak
     to the rest of the page. */
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-course) {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 9pt;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-title) {
    margin: 0 0 2mm;
    font-size: 16pt;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-facts) {
    margin: 0 0 4mm;
    font-size: 10pt;
  }

  /* The masthead an empty sheet prints instead of its facts line: two labels
     with a rule running off each of them, which is the same treatment the
     no-script blank sheet above uses and the same thing a paper form does.
     0.4pt is the table's own hairline, so the rules to write on and the ruled
     rows under them are one weight. */
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-blanks) {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10mm;
    margin: 0 0 4mm;
    font-size: 10pt;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-blank) {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 2mm;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-blank)::after {
    content: "";
    flex: 1;
    border-block-end: 0.4pt solid #000;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-table) {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 10pt;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-table :is(th, td)) {
    padding: 1.6mm 2mm;
    border: 0.4pt solid #000;
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-table thead th) {
    font-size: 8pt;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* Stated because the theme is still in force on this element and prints a
     gold fill behind the header row, a tint behind every second row, and gold
     headings. Simulated the print rules on screen to find it: the record came
     out as a block of brand colour with the day inside it. A sheet somebody
     prints is ink they paid for, and this one is a form. */
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-title) {
    color: #000;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-table tr),
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-table :is(th, td)) {
    color: #000;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-col-time),
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-col-span) {
    width: 18mm;
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-col-mark) {
    width: 26mm;
  }

  /* A row with nothing in it is a row to write in, so it is given the height a
     hand needs rather than collapsing to its own padding. Seven millimetres is
     the line spacing of ordinary ruled paper.

     The whole descendant selector goes inside :global(), including the td.
     Written as :global(.sheet-row-blank) td it compiles with the scope
     attribute on the td, which the script built and which therefore does not
     carry one, and the rule silently matches nothing: the rows came out 12.8px
     tall, which is their padding. Every other rule for this sheet wraps the
     whole selector for the same reason. */
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-row-blank td) {
    height: 7mm;
  }

  /* A block can hold more than one line now, so the print has to keep them. */
  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-col-what) {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
  }

  .recorder-sheet :global(.sheet-foot) {
    margin: 6mm 0 0;
    padding-block-start: 2mm;
    border-block-start: 0.4pt solid #000;
    font-size: 8pt;
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Wiring only. What the sheet knows is in the DOM: the wake time is the first
  // row's field, and every block carries its own length in data-mins, so the
  // clock times are derived rather than stored twice. There is no model held
  // alongside it, because a second copy of a student's day is a second thing
  // that could be persisted by accident, and because the printed sheet is
  // rebuilt from the fields rather than from a cache of them.

  const MINUTES_IN_DAY = 1440;

  // A block has to have a length, and that is the whole of the rule. It was
  // five minutes while the drag snapped to quarter hours; now that a pixel is
  // a minute there is no reason for the sheet to hold an opinion about how
  // short a real block can be.
  const MIN_BLOCK = 1;

  // Reassigned by every wiring pass and read by the listeners that live on
  // window and document. Those cannot be registered inside the pass: window
  // outlives a ClientRouter swap while the document does not, so a listener
  // registered per pass would accumulate one copy per navigation, each holding
  // a detached copy of the sheet.
  let recorder: HTMLElement | null = null;

  // The drag in progress, if any. One at a time by construction: the grip
  // captures the pointer.
  let drag: {
    id: number;
    startPageY: number;
    clientY: number;
    block: HTMLLIElement | null;
    grip: HTMLElement;
    frame: number;
  } | null = null;

  const blocksOf = (root: HTMLElement): HTMLLIElement[] =>
    Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLLIElement>("[data-block]"));

  const timeOf = (block: Element): HTMLInputElement =>
    block.querySelector("[data-time]") as HTMLInputElement;

  const whatOf = (block: Element): HTMLTextAreaElement =>
    block.querySelector("[data-what]") as HTMLTextAreaElement;

  const flagOf = (block: Element): HTMLInputElement =>
    block.querySelector("[data-flag]") as HTMLInputElement;

  const timelineOf = (root: HTMLElement): HTMLElement =>
    root.querySelector("[data-timeline]") as HTMLElement;

  const endTimeOf = (root: HTMLElement): HTMLInputElement =>
    root.querySelector("[data-end-time]") as HTMLInputElement;

  const fieldOf = (
    root: HTMLElement,
    name: string,
  ): HTMLInputElement | HTMLTextAreaElement | HTMLSelectElement | null =>
    root.querySelector(`[data-field="${name}"]`);

  const minsOf = (block: Element): number => Number((block as HTMLElement).dataset.mins) || 0;

  const setMins = (block: Element, minutes: number): void => {
    (block as HTMLElement).dataset.mins = String(Math.max(MIN_BLOCK, Math.round(minutes)));
  };

  const isClosed = (root: HTMLElement): boolean =>
    timelineOf(root).dataset.closed === "true";

  const totalMins = (root: HTMLElement): number =>
    blocksOf(root).reduce((sum, block) => sum + minsOf(block), 0);

  /** "07:15" to 435. Null for an empty or half-typed field. */
  const parseClock = (value: string): number | null => {
    const found = /^(\d{1,2}):(\d{2})$/.exec(value.trim());
    if (!found) return null;
    const minutes = Number(found[1]) * 60 + Number(found[2]);
    return minutes >= 0 && minutes < MINUTES_IN_DAY ? minutes : null;
  };

  const formatClock = (minutes: number): string => {
    const wrapped = ((minutes % MINUTES_IN_DAY) + MINUTES_IN_DAY) % MINUTES_IN_DAY;
    return `${String(Math.floor(wrapped / 60)).padStart(2, "0")}:${String(wrapped % 60).padStart(2, "0")}`;
  };

  const plural = (count: number, word: string): string =>
    `${count} ${word}${count === 1 ? "" : "s"}`;

  const formatSpan = (minutes: number): string => {
    const hours = Math.floor(minutes / 60);
    const rest = minutes % 60;
    if (hours === 0) return `${rest} min`;
    if (rest === 0) return `${hours} h`;
    return `${hours} h ${rest} min`;
  };

  // How long a block is when it is opened without a drag: by the button, by
  // Enter, or by a tap on the line. A resolution select used to set this, back
  // when it also set the drag's snap; per-minute dragging left it with one job
  // and it has been removed, so the length is fixed here.
  //
  // Half an hour, for a reason rather than as a middle value. It is the
  // drawing's floor: the shortest block the timeline can still draw to scale.
  // An undragged block therefore opens at the smallest size that is not a lie
  // about its own height, and the lab's own range, fifteen minutes is plenty
  // and an hour is not useless, sits either side of it.
  const DEFAULT_BLOCK = 30;

  /** Which field holds the wake time: the first block's, or the end row's when
   * no block has been drawn yet. Both are the same line on the drawing. */
  const wakeInputOf = (root: HTMLElement): HTMLInputElement => {
    const first = blocksOf(root)[0];
    return first ? timeOf(first) : endTimeOf(root);
  };

  const wakeMins = (root: HTMLElement): number =>
    parseClock(wakeInputOf(root).value) ?? 7 * 60;

  const announce = (root: HTMLElement, message: string): void => {
    const status = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-status]");
    if (status) status.textContent = message;
  };

  /** Anything a student would be sorry to lose. Recomputed rather than
   * tracked, so clearing the last field also clears the unload warning. A
   * drawn day counts even with nothing written in it. */
  const hasContent = (root: HTMLElement): boolean => {
    if (blocksOf(root).length > 0) return true;
    const date = fieldOf(root, "date") as HTMLInputElement | null;
    return (date?.value.trim().length ?? 0) > 0;
  };

  /**
   * Redraw everything derived: the clock times, the heights, the labels, the
   * totals and the printable sheet. Nothing here decides anything; the
   * durations in data-mins and the wake time are the whole state.
   */
  const layout = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    const blocks = blocksOf(root);
    const closed = isClosed(root);
    const wake = wakeMins(root);
    const end = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-end]");

    let offset = 0;
    blocks.forEach((block, index) => {
      const input = timeOf(block);
      const label = block.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-time-label]");
      if (label) label.textContent = index === 0 ? "Woke at" : "Block starts at";
      block.classList.toggle("is-wake", index === 0);
      const start = formatClock(wake + offset);
      // Never while it is being typed into: writing to a time input resets
      // which segment the caret is in, and a field that jumps back to the hour
      // on every keystroke cannot be typed into at all.
      if (document.activeElement !== input) input.value = start;
      input.dataset.clock = start;
      block.style.setProperty("--mins", String(minsOf(block)));
      block.classList.toggle("is-marked", flagOf(block).checked);
      offset += minsOf(block);
    });

    const endInput = endTimeOf(root);
    const endClock = formatClock(wake + offset);
    if (document.activeElement !== endInput) endInput.value = endClock;
    endInput.dataset.clock = endClock;
    endInput.readOnly = closed;
    end?.classList.toggle("is-wake", blocks.length === 0);
    const endLabel = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-end-label]");
    if (endLabel) {
      endLabel.textContent =
        blocks.length === 0 ? "Woke at" : closed ? "Back to waking at" : "Up to";
    }

    const grip = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-grip]");
    if (grip) grip.hidden = closed;
    const openButton = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-action='open']");
    const sleepButton = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-action='sleep']");
    const reopenButton = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-action='reopen']");
    if (openButton) openButton.hidden = closed;
    if (sleepButton) sleepButton.hidden = closed;
    if (reopenButton) reopenButton.hidden = !closed;

    // The figures: three cells.
    //
    // Coverage leads. It is the week's own spec line, twenty-four continuous
    // hours, and it is the only figure here that cannot be improved by
    // recording differently: splitting one block into three changes the count
    // and leaves the coverage where it was. Its cell is the meter, filling
    // from the left as the hours get accounted for, so an untouched sheet is
    // an empty box and a day with nothing left over is a full one.
    //
    // That fill is the coverage and nothing else, which took checking, because
    // a full cell looks like a closed day and one direction of that is true.
    // Closing the day does always fill it: closeDay hands the sleep block
    // exactly remainingMins, and nothing available afterwards changes the
    // total, since the grip is hidden, the end time is read only, retime
    // trades minutes between two blocks and removeBlock gives a removed
    // block's minutes to its neighbour. The other direction fails. An open day
    // can reach twenty-four hours three ways: a drag clamps to the room left
    // rather than refusing it, retime clamps a last line the same way, and the
    // half hour button takes whatever is left when less than half an hour
    // remains. So nothing here is allowed to read the fill as the state, and
    // nothing here reports the state at all. The sheet does: the buttons swap,
    // the grip goes, the end row relabels to "Back to waking at" and locks,
    // and a sleep block appears with the word in it. All four are set from the
    // same `closed` a few lines above, and layout() runs after every mutation,
    // so they cannot disagree with it. The announcement carries it too, in a
    // whole sentence, which is what a screen reader gets instead of a pill.
    //
    // The other two cells are counts and are written as counts. Eleven blocks
    // is not better than six. The ticks are a share of what is on the sheet
    // rather than a bare number, which is what the author asked for and is
    // also the reading that cannot turn into a target: the denominator moves
    // every time a block opens, so there is no number to get to.
    //
    // Both copies of the coverage cell's text are written here. The second is
    // the one clipped to the fill, and writing them through one call is what
    // keeps them the same words.
    const marked = blocks.filter((block) => flagOf(block).checked).length;
    const figures = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-tally-figures]");
    // A sheet with nothing on it gets no row at all. Three cells reading
    // nought, nought and nought of nought is a scorecard of not having
    // started, and the third one is not even arithmetic.
    const drawn = blocks.length > 0;
    if (figures) figures.hidden = !drawn;

    const write = (selector: string, text: string): void => {
      for (const node of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(selector)) node.textContent = text;
    };

    // The export button says whether there is anything to export. "Nothing
    // recorded yet." was a line of its own under the drawing and is the
    // button's label now, on the author's instruction, and the gold on its
    // border arrives with the first block.
    //
    // The accessible name follows the visible words rather than replacing
    // them, which is the trade this needed. An aria-label reading "Save it as
    // a PDF" over a button that visibly says something else would leave a
    // voice control user saying what they can see and hitting nothing, which
    // is WCAG 2.5.3 and a worse failure than the one it fixes. So the visible
    // words come first and the action is appended in the clipped span, giving
    // "Nothing recorded yet. Save it as a PDF." That span says only what the
    // button does and promises nothing about what comes out of it.
    const printWord = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-print-word]");
    const printTail = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-print-tail]");
    const printButton = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-action='print']");
    if (printWord) printWord.textContent = drawn ? "Save it as a PDF" : "Nothing recorded yet.";
    if (printTail) printTail.textContent = drawn ? "" : " Save it as a PDF.";
    if (printButton) printButton.dataset.ready = String(drawn);

    write("[data-tally-covered]", formatSpan(offset));
    const day = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-tally-day]");
    if (day) {
      day.style.setProperty("--covered", `${((offset / MINUTES_IN_DAY) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`);
    }

    write("[data-tally-blocks]", String(blocks.length));
    write("[data-tally-blocks-label]", blocks.length === 1 ? "block drawn" : "blocks drawn");
    write("[data-tally-marked]", `${marked} of ${blocks.length}`);

    renderSheet(root);
  };

  /** A small element, with its text set safely. */
  const make = (tag: string, className: string, text?: string): HTMLElement => {
    const node = document.createElement(tag);
    if (className) node.className = className;
    if (text !== undefined) node.textContent = text;
    return node;
  };

  /**
   * The printed sheet, rebuilt from the fields. Rebuilt on every change rather
   * than on beforeprint alone: beforeprint is not reliable everywhere, and a
   * student who reaches for Ctrl+P instead of the button must not get last
   * minute's day. It is cheap, it is a dozen elements.
   *
   * textContent throughout, never innerHTML. What goes in here is what a
   * student typed about their own day.
   */
  const renderSheet = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    const sheet = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-sheet]");
    if (!sheet) return;
    const blocks = blocksOf(root);
    const closed = isClosed(root);
    const wake = wakeMins(root);
    const total = totalMins(root);

    const date = (fieldOf(root, "date") as HTMLInputElement | null)?.value ?? "";

    // The header, which is two different things depending on whether there is
    // anything to report.
    //
    // A sheet with nothing on it prints as a form: two labelled rules, for the
    // date and for the waking time, because that sheet is going to be filled
    // in with a pen and the pen needs somewhere to put them. What it must not
    // print is the facts line, which on an empty sheet reads "Woke at: 07:00 ·
    // Covers: 0 min since waking": the first asserts a default nobody typed
    // and the second announces nought of twenty-four hours on a piece of paper
    // where it cannot be taken back. That is the scorecard failure week 4
    // writes a stopping rule for, printed.
    const header = blocks.length === 0 ? make("p", "sheet-blanks") : make("p", "sheet-facts");
    if (blocks.length === 0) {
      header.append(make("span", "sheet-blank", "Date"), make("span", "sheet-blank", "Woke at"));
    } else {
      header.textContent = [
        `Date: ${date || "not stated"}`,
        `Woke at: ${formatClock(wake)}`,
        closed
          ? `Covers: 24 h in ${plural(blocks.length, "block")}, sleep included`
          : `Covers: ${formatSpan(total)} since waking, and the day is not closed`,
      ].join("  ·  ");
    }

    const table = make("table", "sheet-table");
    const head = document.createElement("thead");
    const headRow = document.createElement("tr");
    for (const [label, className] of [
      ["Starts", "sheet-col-time"],
      ["Runs for", "sheet-col-span"],
      ["What I was doing", "sheet-col-what"],
      ["Might change", "sheet-col-mark"],
    ] as const) {
      const cell = make("th", className, label);
      cell.setAttribute("scope", "col");
      headRow.append(cell);
    }
    head.append(headRow);
    table.append(head);

    const body = document.createElement("tbody");
    let offset = 0;
    for (const block of blocks) {
      const row = document.createElement("tr");
      const mins = minsOf(block);
      row.append(make("td", "sheet-col-time", formatClock(wake + offset)));
      row.append(make("td", "sheet-col-span", formatSpan(mins)));
      // The sleep block is the one row the student did not type, so it says so
      // rather than printing an empty cell that reads as an unrecorded hour.
      const what = whatOf(block).value.trim();
      row.append(
        make("td", "sheet-col-what", what || (block.dataset.sleep === "true" ? "asleep" : "")),
      );
      // The word, not a tick or a colour: this sheet is printed in black and
      // read on paper, and "marked" survives both.
      row.append(make("td", "sheet-col-mark", flagOf(block).checked ? "marked" : ""));
      body.append(row);
      offset += mins;
    }

    // Ruled room to carry on, while the day is still open.
    //
    // The instruction was to pad the empty sheet, which printed a header over
    // no rows at all and was useless to anybody who wanted paper. The rule
    // implemented is wider than that and narrower than "pad whenever there are
    // fewer rows than the template", which was the other candidate. It pads
    // while the day is open, at any number of blocks, and never once it is
    // closed.
    //
    // Open is the state that means the record is unfinished, so ruled rows are
    // an invitation to write the rest, and the empty sheet is just that case at
    // zero. Closed means the opposite by construction: pressing Asleep from
    // here fills the twenty-four hours, so the record is complete, and blank
    // rows under a sheet whose own header says it covers 24 h would be the
    // printed page contradicting itself and asking for hours that do not exist.
    //
    // The count comes from the blank paper sheet in the markup rather than from
    // a second copy of the number, so the printed template and the no-script
    // one cannot drift apart. They are meant to be the same document.
    if (!closed) {
      const paperRows = root.querySelectorAll("[data-paper] tbody tr").length || 14;
      for (let index = blocks.length; index < paperRows; index += 1) {
        const row = document.createElement("tr");
        row.className = "sheet-row-blank";
        // Every column a filled row has, the tick column included, so the sheet
        // somebody continues with a pen is the same document as the one the
        // script wrote the first rows of.
        for (const className of [
          "sheet-col-time",
          "sheet-col-span",
          "sheet-col-what",
          "sheet-col-mark",
        ]) {
          row.append(make("td", className, ""));
        }
        body.append(row);
      }
    }

    table.append(body);

    sheet.replaceChildren(
      make("p", "sheet-course", "SLOP1638 week 1 lab · baseline record"),
      make("h2", "sheet-title", "One day, recorded and not improved"),
      header,
      table,
      make(
        "p",
        "sheet-foot",
        "Written on the week 1 day sheet, which saved no copy of any of this.",
      ),
    );
  };

  /** How much of the day is still unspoken for. */
  const remainingMins = (root: HTMLElement): number => MINUTES_IN_DAY - totalMins(root);

  /** Open a block at the end of the day. Returns null when the twenty-four
   * hours are already full, which is the one thing that can stop it. */
  const openBlock = (root: HTMLElement, minutes: number, sleep = false): HTMLLIElement | null => {
    const room = remainingMins(root);
    if (room < MIN_BLOCK) {
      announce(root, "The twenty-four hours are full. Shorten a block, or close the day.");
      return null;
    }
    const template = root.querySelector<HTMLTemplateElement>("[data-block-template]");
    const model = template?.content.firstElementChild;
    if (!model) return null;
    const block = model.cloneNode(true) as HTMLLIElement;
    const wakeBefore = wakeMins(root);
    setMins(block, Math.min(Math.max(minutes, MIN_BLOCK), room));
    if (sleep) {
      block.dataset.sleep = "true";
      whatOf(block).value = "asleep";
      block.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-action='remove']")?.remove();
    }
    root.querySelector("[data-blocks]")?.append(block);
    // The first block takes over the wake time from the end row, which was
    // holding it while the sheet had no blocks at all.
    if (blocksOf(root).length === 1) timeOf(block).value = formatClock(wakeBefore);
    layout(root);
    return block;
  };

  /** Remove a block and give its minutes to a neighbour, so the wake time and
   * the end of the day both stay where they were. */
  const removeBlock = (root: HTMLElement, block: HTMLLIElement): void => {
    const blocks = blocksOf(root);
    const index = blocks.indexOf(block);
    const mins = minsOf(block);
    // The wake time lives in whichever field is first on the sheet, so a
    // removal that changes which field that is has to carry it across. Without
    // this, deleting the first block promoted the second block's start time to
    // be the wake time, and deleting the only block left the end row holding
    // the end of the day and called it waking: a 07:00 sheet with one four
    // hour block came back as a sheet that started at 11:00.
    const wakeBefore = wakeMins(root);
    const previous = blocks[index - 1];
    const next = blocks[index + 1];
    const started = timeOf(block).value;
    // Focus has to land somewhere before the element holding it leaves the
    // document, or it falls to the body and a keyboard user starts the tab
    // order again from the top of the page.
    const landing =
      (next && whatOf(next)) ||
      (previous && whatOf(previous)) ||
      root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-action='open']");
    landing?.focus();
    if (previous) setMins(previous, minsOf(previous) + mins);
    else if (next) setMins(next, minsOf(next) + mins);
    block.remove();
    const first = blocksOf(root)[0];
    (first ? timeOf(first) : endTimeOf(root)).value = formatClock(wakeBefore);
    layout(root);
    announce(
      root,
      blocksOf(root).length === 0
        ? "Block removed. The sheet is back to the waking line."
        : `Block starting ${started} removed, and its ${formatSpan(mins)} went to the block ${previous ? "above" : "below"} it.`,
    );
  };

  /** Close the day: everything from the last line back around to waking is
   * sleep. This is what turns a waking day into the twenty-four continuous
   * hours the lab asks for, and it is why the sheet never reports a gap. */
  const closeDay = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    const blocks = blocksOf(root);
    if (blocks.length === 0) {
      announce(root, "Draw at least one block before you close the day.");
      return;
    }
    const sleep = remainingMins(root);
    if (sleep < MIN_BLOCK) {
      announce(root, "There is no time left to sleep in: the blocks already fill the day.");
      return;
    }
    const wake = wakeMins(root);
    const from = formatClock(wake + totalMins(root));
    timelineOf(root).dataset.closed = "true";
    const block = openBlock(root, sleep, true);
    layout(root);
    if (block) whatOf(block).focus();
    announce(
      root,
      `Day closed. ${from} back around to ${formatClock(wake)} is recorded as asleep, ${formatSpan(sleep)}. The sheet now covers twenty-four hours.`,
    );
  };

  const reopenDay = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    const blocks = blocksOf(root);
    const last = blocks[blocks.length - 1];
    if (last?.dataset.sleep === "true") last.remove();
    delete timelineOf(root).dataset.closed;
    layout(root);
    root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-action='open']")?.focus();
    announce(root, "Day reopened. The sleep block is gone and the line is loose again.");
  };

  /** A boundary was typed or arrowed into a new time. Move that one line and
   * leave its neighbours where they are: the block above takes the difference
   * and the block below gives it up. */
  const retime = (root: HTMLElement, input: HTMLInputElement): void => {
    const now = parseClock(input.value);
    const before = parseClock(input.dataset.clock ?? "");
    if (now === null || before === null) return;
    // Nearest wrap, so typing 23:45 under a block that started at 00:15 reads
    // as half an hour earlier rather than as most of a day later.
    let delta = ((now - before + MINUTES_IN_DAY + 720) % MINUTES_IN_DAY) - 720;
    if (delta === 0) return;

    const blocks = blocksOf(root);
    const block = input.closest<HTMLLIElement>("[data-block]");

    // The wake line. Everything keeps its length and the whole day slides.
    if (!block && blocks.length === 0) {
      layout(root);
      return;
    }
    if (block && blocks.indexOf(block) === 0) {
      layout(root);
      return;
    }

    const above = block ? blocks[blocks.indexOf(block) - 1] : blocks[blocks.length - 1];
    // Which block pays for the move. An interior line has blocks on both sides
    // and the later ones are already placed, so the two it sits between trade
    // the difference. The last line of an open day has nothing after it, so
    // moving it moves the end of the day and the block below keeps its length:
    // typing 22:45 on the line under a nine hour evening means the evening
    // ended at 22:45, not that the five minutes after it have to absorb three
    // hours. That clamp is what produced a sheet whose times and lengths
    // disagreed, and it produced it silently.
    const last = blocks[blocks.length - 1];
    const trades = block && !(block === last && !isClosed(root));
    const below = trades ? block : null;
    if (!above) return;

    let clamped = false;
    if (minsOf(above) + delta < MIN_BLOCK) {
      delta = MIN_BLOCK - minsOf(above);
      clamped = true;
    }
    if (below && minsOf(below) - delta < MIN_BLOCK) {
      delta = minsOf(below) - MIN_BLOCK;
      clamped = true;
    }
    if (!below && totalMins(root) + delta > MINUTES_IN_DAY) {
      delta = MINUTES_IN_DAY - totalMins(root);
      clamped = true;
    }

    setMins(above, minsOf(above) + delta);
    if (below) setMins(below, minsOf(below) - delta);
    layout(root);
    if (clamped) {
      announce(
        root,
        `Kept at ${input.value}: a block cannot be shorter than a minute, and the day cannot run past twenty-four hours.`,
      );
    }
  };

  // The drag itself. Measured in page coordinates rather than client ones, so
  // that scrolling the window under a still finger goes on lengthening the
  // block: the autoscroll below depends on it.
  const dragFrame = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    if (!drag) return;
    const zone = 90;
    if (drag.clientY > window.innerHeight - zone) window.scrollBy(0, 14);
    else if (drag.clientY < zone) window.scrollBy(0, -14);

    const pulled = drag.clientY + window.scrollY - drag.startPageY;
    if (pulled > 3 || drag.block) {
      // A pixel is a minute and nothing rounds it. The sheet used to snap to
      // the resolution, which meant it could not record that somebody got up
      // at 07:43: the lab asks for the day as it was, and quarter hours are
      // how finely a student needs to write it down, not how precisely the
      // instrument is allowed to let them.
      const room = remainingMins(root) + (drag.block ? minsOf(drag.block) : 0);
      const minutes = Math.min(Math.max(Math.round(pulled), MIN_BLOCK), Math.max(MIN_BLOCK, room));
      if (!drag.block) drag.block = openBlock(root, minutes);
      else {
        setMins(drag.block, minutes);
        layout(root);
      }
      const readout = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-readout]");
      if (readout && drag.block) {
        const wake = wakeMins(root);
        readout.textContent = `${formatClock(wake + totalMins(root))} · ${formatSpan(minsOf(drag.block))}`;
      }
    }
    drag.frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => dragFrame(root));
  };

  const endDrag = (root: HTMLElement, commit: boolean): void => {
    if (!drag) return;
    cancelAnimationFrame(drag.frame);
    const block = drag.block;
    const grip = drag.grip;
    drag = null;
    timelineOf(root).classList.remove("is-dragging");
    const readout = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-readout]");
    if (readout) readout.textContent = "";
    if (!commit) return;
    // A tap with no pull is the single-pointer path that WCAG 2.5.7 asks for:
    // half an hour, the same as the button beside it.
    const opened = block ?? openBlock(root, DEFAULT_BLOCK);
    if (!opened) return;
    whatOf(opened).focus();
    const wake = wakeMins(root);
    const start = formatClock(wake + totalMins(root) - minsOf(opened));
    announce(
      root,
      `Block opened, ${start} to ${formatClock(wake + totalMins(root))}, ${formatSpan(minsOf(opened))}. Say what you were doing.`,
    );
    void grip;
  };

  function setUpDayRecorder(): void {
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-day-recorder]");
    recorder = root;
    if (!root) return;
    // Already wired: this is a navigation that kept the element rather than
    // replacing it, so there is nothing to bind and only the derived text to
    // put back.
    if (root.dataset.scripted === "true") {
      layout(root);
      return;
    }
    // Also what hides the blank paper sheet, so the fallback is visible
    // exactly when this line has not run.
    root.dataset.scripted = "true";

    root.addEventListener("input", (event) => {
      const target = event.target as HTMLElement | null;
      if (target?.matches("[data-time], [data-end-time]")) {
        retime(root, target as HTMLInputElement);
        return;
      }
      layout(root);
    });
    root.addEventListener("change", () => layout(root));

    // layout() never writes to the field somebody is typing in, so a value it
    // had to correct stays on screen until focus moves. This is the moment it
    // moves: one frame later the caret is somewhere else and the field can be
    // told what it actually holds.
    root.addEventListener("focusout", () => requestAnimationFrame(() => layout(root)));

    // Enter walks the two fields that carry the day and opens the next block
    // at the end, so the whole thing can be typed. It only ever walks forward:
    // inserting in the middle belongs to the line above the block, which is a
    // time you can type into.
    root.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
      if (!(event instanceof KeyboardEvent) || event.key !== "Enter") return;
      // The writing area is a textarea, so Enter had to be taken off it to keep
      // walking the day. Shift and Enter is the newline, which is the
      // convention every message box has taught, and it is said on the page
      // rather than left to be discovered.
      if (event.shiftKey) return;
      const target = event.target as HTMLElement | null;
      if (!target) return;
      if (target.matches("[data-time]")) {
        event.preventDefault();
        const block = target.closest<HTMLLIElement>("[data-block]");
        if (block) whatOf(block).focus();
        return;
      }
      if (target.matches("[data-end-time]")) {
        event.preventDefault();
        root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-action='open']")?.focus();
        return;
      }
      if (!target.matches("[data-what]")) return;
      event.preventDefault();
      const block = target.closest<HTMLLIElement>("[data-block]");
      const next = block?.nextElementSibling as HTMLLIElement | null;
      if (next?.matches("[data-block]")) {
        timeOf(next).focus();
        return;
      }
      if (isClosed(root)) return;
      const opened = openBlock(root, DEFAULT_BLOCK);
      if (!opened) return;
      timeOf(opened).focus();
      announce(root, `Block opened, ${formatSpan(minsOf(opened))}. Correct the start time or press Enter again.`);
    });

    root.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
      const button = (event.target as HTMLElement | null)?.closest<HTMLButtonElement>(
        "[data-action]",
      );
      if (!button) return;
      const action = button.dataset.action;

      if (action === "open") {
        const opened = openBlock(root, DEFAULT_BLOCK);
        if (!opened) return;
        whatOf(opened).focus();
        announce(
          root,
          `Block opened, ${formatSpan(minsOf(opened))}. Say what you were doing, and correct the time on the line above it if the length is wrong.`,
        );
        return;
      }

      if (action === "remove") {
        const block = button.closest<HTMLLIElement>("[data-block]");
        if (block) removeBlock(root, block);
        return;
      }

      if (action === "sleep") {
        closeDay(root);
        return;
      }

      if (action === "reopen") {
        reopenDay(root);
        return;
      }

      if (action === "print") {
        renderSheet(root);
        announce(
          root,
          "Sheet sent to the print dialogue. Nothing was saved: the file you keep is the only copy.",
        );
        window.print();
      }
    });

    const grip = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-grip]");
    if (grip) {
      grip.addEventListener("pointerdown", (event) => {
        if (!(event instanceof PointerEvent) || isClosed(root)) return;
        // Stops the browser starting a text selection or a scroll of its own.
        // The grip is the only element in the component with touch-action:
        // none, so this is the only place the page gives up its own gesture.
        event.preventDefault();
        // Capture so the pull survives the pointer leaving the grip, which it
        // does immediately: the grip moves down with the line while the finger
        // stays where it is. Guarded because capture throws on a pointer id the
        // browser does not have active, which is what a synthetic event is.
        try {
          grip.setPointerCapture(event.pointerId);
        } catch {
          /* uncaptured is still workable: the listeners are on the grip */
        }
        timelineOf(root).classList.add("is-dragging");
        drag = {
          id: event.pointerId,
          // clientY plus the scroll offset rather than pageY, so the start of
          // the pull is measured the same way every frame of it is. It also
          // keeps the handler testable: a synthetic PointerEvent carries a
          // pageY of 0, which made a 95px pull read as a five hour block.
          startPageY: event.clientY + window.scrollY,
          clientY: event.clientY,
          block: null,
          grip,
          frame: 0,
        };
        drag.frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => dragFrame(root));
      });

      grip.addEventListener("pointermove", (event) => {
        if (!drag || !(event instanceof PointerEvent) || event.pointerId !== drag.id) return;
        drag.clientY = event.clientY;
      });

      grip.addEventListener("pointerup", () => endDrag(root, true));
      grip.addEventListener("pointercancel", () => endDrag(root, false));
    }

    layout(root);
  }

  // A page that saves nothing owes a warning before it throws the lot away.
  // This is the one case where beforeunload is not user hostile: the work is
  // genuinely unrecoverable, and it fires only when there is something to lose.
  window.addEventListener("beforeunload", (event) => {
    if (!recorder?.isConnected || !hasContent(recorder)) return;
    event.preventDefault();
  });

  // ClientRouter navigations never reach beforeunload: the router swaps the
  // document in place. Cancelling its preparation event is what routes them
  // back through the browser, since Astro falls back to assigning
  // location.href when a listener prevents the default, and that is an
  // ordinary navigation, which does fire beforeunload. One warning, the
  // browser's own, on both paths.
  document.addEventListener("astro:before-preparation", (event) => {
    if (!recorder?.isConnected || !hasContent(recorder)) return;
    event.preventDefault();
  });

  // Ctrl+P and the print menu skip the button, so the sheet is refreshed here
  // too, and the status line is what tells a reader afterwards that the page
  // still holds the only copy.
  window.addEventListener("beforeprint", () => {
    if (recorder?.isConnected) renderSheet(recorder);
  });

  window.addEventListener("afterprint", () => {
    if (recorder?.isConnected) {
      announce(
        recorder,
        "Print dialogue closed. Nothing was saved here: if you did not keep the file, the record is still only in this tab.",
      );
    }
  });

  setUpDayRecorder();
  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", setUpDayRecorder);
</script>
